The videogame was developed using C# programming language inside Unity
, applying good programming practices and a structured code organization.
The source code was written entirely in English, including variables, functions, classes and comments
, making the project easier to understand, maintain and expand.
The project was divided into different scripts according to their functionality:
•	PlayerController.cs → Controls player movement, jumping, animations, sounds and collisions. 
•	GameManager.cs → Manages score system, victory, game over and UI panels. 
•	MainMenuController.cs → Controls the main menu navigation and scene loading. 
Several good programming practices were implemented during development, including:
•	Meaningful variable names: 
movementSpeed
jumpForce
currentScore
horizontalInput
isGrounded
•	Clear and organized function names: 
MovePlayer()
Jump()
UpdateAnimations()
TriggerGameOver()
TriggerVictory()
RestartGame()
LoadMainMenu()
•	Descriptive comments explaining the functionality of each section of the code: 
// Initialize required components
// Handle jumping mechanism
// Detect collision with collectibles
// Freeze game physics
// Load the main menu scene
•	Modular code structure separating gameplay systems into independent methods. 
•	Use of the Singleton design pattern in GameManager to ensure only one manager instance exists during gameplay. 
•	Validation checks to prevent runtime errors: 
if (jumpSound != null)
if (scoreText != null)
if (!isGameActive) return;
•	Organized Unity inspector configuration using headers and serialized fields: 
[Header("Movement Settings")]
[SerializeField] private float movementSpeed = 6.0f;
These practices improved readability, 
organization and maintainability of the project,
 allowing the team to develop a stable and functional final prototype of Mario Lava Game.
